        "heading": "The Copilot record: where it is born and what the envelope says",
        "content": "### Where the record is born\n\nMicrosoft 365 writes one shared log for its services, called the unified audit log. It belongs to Microsoft Purview, Microsoft's compliance suite. Microsoft documents that Copilot activities are logged automatically as part of Audit (Standard): if auditing is enabled for the organisation, no extra step is needed. Audit log search is turned on by default for Microsoft 365 and Office 365 enterprise organisations.\n\nWhen a user sends a prompt to Copilot and gets an answer, the service writes a record whose `Operation` is `CopilotInteraction`. A single audit record typically contains a prompt-response pair, and it can also hold one prompt with several responses.\n\n### The envelope\n\nThe outer fields are called the common schema. Every Microsoft 365 audit record has them.\n\n| Field | Meaning | Analyst note |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Id` | Unique identifier of the audit record | Quote it in the ticket |\n| `CreationTime` | Date and time in UTC (Coordinated Universal Time) when the record was generated | Convert before comparing with local-time sources |\n| `Operation` | Name of the activity | `CopilotInteraction` for user prompts |\n| `RecordType` | Numeric category of the record | `261` is CopilotInteraction |\n| `Workload` | Service where the activity occurred | `Copilot` |\n| `UserId` | UPN (user principal name, the sign-in name that looks like an email address) | Changes if the user is renamed |\n| `UserKey` | An alternative ID for the same user | Keep it beside `UserId` |\n| `UserType` | Kind of account | `0` is a regular user, `2` is an administrator |\n| `ClientIP` | IPv4 or IPv6 address of the device that was used | See the pitfall below |\n| `OrganizationId` | GUID (globally unique identifier) of the tenant | The same on every record of the tenant |\n\n### Three record types that look alike\n\n| RecordType | Number | What it covers |\n|---|---|---|\n| `CopilotInteraction` | 261 | Copilot applications developed by Microsoft |\n| `ConnectedAIAppInteraction` | 328 | Custom-built or third-party AI applications deployed and registered in the organisation |\n| `AIAppInteraction` | 284 | Third-party AI applications that are not deployed in the organisation |\n\nThe matching `Workload` values are `Copilot`, `ConnectedAIApp` and `AIApp`. Audit logs for non-Microsoft AI applications use pay-as-you-go billing and have to be enabled, so an empty result for `AIAppInteraction` does not prove that nobody used such a tool.\n\n### The ClientIP pitfall\n\nMicrosoft documents that for some services the value in `ClientIP` might be the IP address of a trusted application calling into the service on behalf of a user, not the address of the device the person used. Treat `ClientIP` as a lead. Confirm the location in the sign-in log of the IdP (identity provider, the system that checks who is signing in).\n\n### What the analyst does\n\n- Filter on `Operation` or `RecordType` first, then on `UserId` and a time window.\n- Read `CreationTime` as UTC, always.\n- Copy `Id` into your notes so that a colleague can find the same record again.\n- Check `UserType`. A `CopilotInteraction` by an administrator account is still a user prompt, but it tells you whose permissions Copilot was working with.",
