        "heading": "Inside CopilotEventData: app, session, messages and resources",
        "content": "### The AI-specific block\n\nEverything that makes this an AI record sits in a nested object named `CopilotEventData`, together with a few sibling properties such as `AppIdentity` and `ClientRegion`.\n\n| Field | What it tells you |\n|---|---|\n| `AppHost` | The application that hosted the interaction, for example `BizChat`, `Word`, `Teams` or `Outlook` |\n| `AppIdentity` | A string that identifies the specific Copilot or AI application, typically built as workloadName.appGroup.appName |\n| `Contexts` | Where the user was: an `Id` (a file path, a Teams chat or meeting identifier) and a `Type` such as docx or TeamsMeeting |\n| `ThreadId` | The ID of the Copilot and user interaction thread |\n| `Messages` | One entry for each prompt or response: `Id`, `isPrompt`, and `JailbreakDetected` |\n| `AccessedResources` | References to the files, documents and emails Copilot accessed to answer |\n| `AISystemPlugin` | Plugins or extensions used; an `Id` of `BingWebSearch` means the public web was used |\n| `ModelTransparencyDetails` | Details of the AI model, starting with the provider name |\n| `AgentId`, `AgentName` | Identify an agent when one handled the prompt |\n\n### AccessedResources, the field analysts read most\n\nEach entry describes one resource.\n\n- `Name`: the readable name, such as a file name\n- `SiteUrl`: the URL of the resource, for example a SharePoint site or the full path of a file\n- `Id`: a unique identifier, which can be a file identifier, a Teams message identifier or an email identifier\n- `Type`: a file extension or a kind of resource\n- `Action`: what Copilot did, commonly read, create or modify\n- `SensitivityLabelId`: the ID of the sensitivity label assigned to the resource, when it has one\n- `Status`: whether the action on that resource was a success or a failure\n- `PolicyDetails`: filled when a policy blocked or restricted Copilot's access to the resource\n- `XPIADetected`: true or false, whether a cross prompt injection attack (XPIA) was detected from that resource\n\n### Reading Messages correctly\n\n`isPrompt` true marks what the user sent, and false marks what Copilot returned. The `Id` values are message identifiers, not text. `Size` is documented as currently not used.\n\n`JailbreakDetected` is a flag that the product sets on a prompt message. It is the product's classification, not your conclusion. Record the value as an observation and keep investigating either way.\n\n### AppHost is not the device\n\n`AppHost` names the application that hosted the chat. `BizChat` is the Microsoft 365 Copilot Chat client. `Word` is Copilot inside Word. The value says nothing about laptop versus phone, so do not use it as a device indicator.\n\n### What the analyst asks\n\n- How many entries are in `AccessedResources`, and does any of them carry a `SensitivityLabelId`?\n- Does every entry show `Status` success, or was something blocked?\n- Is `Contexts` empty, or does it name the file the user had open?\n- Does the same `ThreadId` appear on earlier records, which would mean one long conversation?\n\nAn empty `AccessedResources` list is ordinary. Microsoft's own sample record for a prompt in Word shows an empty list with a `Contexts` entry for the open document.",
