        "heading": "What the Copilot record leaves out, and where the text lives",
        "content": "### No prompt text in the audit record\n\nThe `CopilotInteraction` record carries message identifiers and resource references. It does not carry the words the user typed or the words Copilot answered. This is the single most common misreading by new analysts, so say it out loud before you write a finding.\n\nA third everyday picture: a phone bill. It lists who called whom, when and for how long. It does not contain the call.\n\n### Where the text is kept\n\nMicrosoft documents that data from generative AI messages is stored in a hidden folder in the mailbox of the user who runs the AI app. The folder is not designed to be directly accessible to users or administrators. It exists so that compliance administrators can search it with eDiscovery (electronic discovery, the tooling used to find and export content for legal or investigative cases).\n\n| Need | Where | Typical role |\n|---|---|---|\n| Who used Copilot, when, with which files | Audit search or the SIEM | Analyst holding the Audit Logs or View-Only Audit Logs role |\n| The words of the prompt and of the answer | eDiscovery search with the user's mailbox as the source | Compliance or eDiscovery role, with a documented reason |\n\n### Two retention clocks\n\nRetention means how long a record is kept before it is deleted. There are two clocks, and they are unrelated.\n\n**Clock one: the audit record.** E5 is Microsoft's top enterprise licence tier. For users with a non-E5 licence, audit records are retained for 180 days. Audit (Premium) has a default policy that keeps records for one year, but only records whose `Workload` is `AzureActiveDirectory`, `Exchange`, `OneDrive` or `SharePoint`. Microsoft states that audit records for all other activities are retained for 180 days by default. A Copilot record has `Workload` `Copilot`, so plan on 180 days unless your organisation created a custom audit log retention policy. An organisation can have up to 50 such policies.\n\n**Clock two: the message text.** It follows retention policies for Copilot and AI apps, which are configured separately. Microsoft warns that messages visible in the app are not an accurate reflection of whether they are retained or permanently deleted for compliance.\n\n### How fast records arrive\n\nMicrosoft does not guarantee a time. For core services such as Exchange, SharePoint, OneDrive and Teams, audit records are typically available 60 to 90 minutes after an event, and for other services it might be longer. A search made ten minutes after an event that returns nothing proves nothing.\n\n### Search pitfalls\n\n- Keyword search in the audit tool looks only at the indexed common schema. It does not search the audit data content, so a value that exists only inside `CopilotEventData` is not found that way.\n- To find records with a specific `AppIdentity`, Microsoft says to export the Copilot records first and filter on that property offline.\n- The maximum date range for one search is 180 days.\n\n### A false conclusion to avoid\n\n\"The record lists Payroll-2026.xlsx in `AccessedResources`, so the user asked about payroll.\" The record shows only that Copilot referenced the file while answering. The prompt could have been a request to summarise the week. If the wording matters, request the text through the compliance route and say in your notes that you have not seen it."
