      {
        "heading": "Where the records land: SIEM tables, collection and retention",
        "content": "### Microsoft 365 Copilot in the SIEM\n\nAudit records can be read in three ways: the Audit search in the Microsoft Purview portal, the `Search-UnifiedAuditLog` cmdlet (a PowerShell command) in Exchange Online PowerShell, and the Office 365 Management Activity API for programmatic download. A SIEM uses the last route or a vendor connector.\n\nIn Microsoft Sentinel, Microsoft's cloud SIEM, the Azure Monitor table reference describes a table named `CopilotActivity` as audit logs for Copilot and other AI workloads. Its columns rename the audit fields:\n\n| Column | Holds |\n|---|---|\n| `TimeGenerated` | Timestamp of the audit event |\n| `ActorName` | User principal name or email address |\n| `ActorUserId` | Internal user key or GUID |\n| `SrcIpAddr` | IP address of the client |\n| `AppHost` | Application that hosts Copilot |\n| `RecordType` | Normalised record type name, for example CopilotInteraction |\n| `LLMEventData` | Parsed event data, of type dynamic |\n\nA dynamic column holds nested JSON. Sentinel is searched with KQL (Kusto Query Language). To reach one resource per row you expand the array with the KQL operator `mv-expand`, as Microsoft's sample queries do for `LLMEventData.AccessedResources` and `LLMEventData.Messages`.\n\n### Claude Enterprise in the SIEM\n\nNothing is pushed. A collector, meaning a small scheduled program, calls the Activity Feed and stores what it gets. Anthropic documents two patterns.\n\n| Pattern | How it works | The trap |\n|---|---|---|\n| Window polling | Ask for a time window with `created_at.gte` and `created_at.lt` | Set `created_at.lt` at least 1 minute in the past, or late-indexed activities are dropped for good |\n| Cursor reads | Save `first_id` and pass it as `before_id` on the next run | Persist the cursor only after `has_more` is false |\n\nTreat the feed as at-least-once: a retry can deliver an activity twice, so deduplicate on the activity `id`. Each page holds at most 5,000 activities.\n\nThe collector leaves its own footprint. Most calls to the Compliance API emit `compliance_api_accessed` activities whose actor is an `api_actor`, and `actor.api_key_id` attributes the access to a specific key. Know your collector's key ID, or its routine polling will look like a stranger reading compliance data.\n\n### Retention side by side\n\n| Assistant | Activity trail | Kept for |\n|---|---|---|\n| Microsoft 365 Copilot | Unified audit log, `CopilotInteraction` | 180 days by default for the Copilot workload |\n| Claude Enterprise | Compliance API Activity Feed | 6 years |\n| ChatGPT Enterprise | Compliance Logs Platform | 30 days |\n| Gemini in Google Workspace | Gemini for Workspace log events | Not stated on the event page: check the tenant |\n\nOpenAI describes its Compliance Logs Platform as immutable, append-only compliance log events, with audit, authentication and app log categories. Google's event page lists attributes such as Actor, Action, Event Category and Feature Source.\n\n### What the analyst does\n\nThe shortest retention sets the deadline. If the SIEM does not copy ChatGPT Enterprise events within 30 days, they are gone. Ask the SIEM owner three things for every AI feed: how often is it pulled, how long does the SIEM keep it, and does an alert fire when the feed goes quiet?",
        "codeExample": "// Sentinel KQL: one row per resource Copilot referenced for one user, last 7 days\nCopilotActivity\n| where RecordType == \"CopilotInteraction\"\n| where TimeGenerated >= ago(7d)\n| where ActorName =~ \"m.adeyemi@kettering-mills.example\"\n| extend AccessedResources = LLMEventData.AccessedResources\n| mv-expand AccessedResources\n| project TimeGenerated, ActorName, SrcIpAddr, AppHost,\n          ResourceName = tostring(AccessedResources.Name),\n          ResourceUrl  = tostring(AccessedResources.SiteUrl),\n          Action       = tostring(AccessedResources.Action)\n| order by TimeGenerated asc"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Pivoting: from user to session to resource",
        "content": "### The idea\n\nOne record answers one moment. An investigation needs the chain: this person, these conversations, these resources. Each link in the chain is a field value that you copy from one record into the next search.\n\n### The chain in Microsoft 365 Copilot\n\n| Step | From | To | Join value |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| User | A name in a ticket | All `CopilotInteraction` records of that account | `UserId`, with `UserKey` as the alternative ID |\n| Session | One record | Every record of the same conversation | `CopilotEventData.ThreadId` |\n| Resource | One conversation | The files and emails it referenced | `AccessedResources[].SiteUrl` and `Id` |\n| Outside the AI log | One file | The file's own audit history | SharePoint `ObjectId` |\n\nThe last step leaves the Copilot record. In SharePoint and OneDrive audit records, `ObjectId` is the full path name of the file or folder that was accessed. It equals the combination of `SiteUrl`, `SourceRelativeUrl` and `SourceFileName`. A `SiteUrl` copied from `AccessedResources` therefore leads to the `FileAccessed` records for the same file, and to everyone else who opened it.\n\n### The chain in Claude Enterprise\n\nAnthropic publishes the join table itself.\n\n| Compliance API field | Join target |\n|---|---|\n| `actor.user_id` | The identity provider's stable user identifier |\n| `actor.email_address` | Directory email when a stable ID is unavailable |\n| `actor.ip_address` | Network, VPN (virtual private network) and endpoint logs |\n| `actor.user_agent` | Endpoint and device inventory, and the client app that made the request |\n| `created_at` | Time-window correlation across any source |\n\n`user_id` does not change when the user's email or display name changes, so Anthropic says to use `user_id`, not `email_address`, as the primary join key. The session link is `claude_chat_id`: every activity about the same chat carries it. The resource link is `claude_file_id` or `claude_project_id`.\n\n### Four pivot mistakes\n\n1. **Joining on a name.** Display names repeat and change. Use `UserKey` or `user_id`.\n2. **Mixing clocks.** `CreationTime` is UTC. A proxy log in local time will look an hour or more out of step.\n3. **Counting views as reading.** Fourteen `claude_chat_viewed` activities in a minute can be one app reloading.\n4. **Reading an actor field that is not there.** `actor.ip_address` and `actor.user_agent` are absent on some actor types. Check `actor.type` first.\n\n### Why the chain matters to security\n\nThe same chain serves two later questions that other lessons develop. If a cloud account is used by someone who stole its credentials (MITRE ATT&CK T1078.004, Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts), the assistant's trail shows which conversations and files that session reached. If someone uses company knowledge stores to gather information (ATT&CK T1213, Data from Information Repositories), `AccessedResources` and the file identifiers are the list of what was gathered. Mapping the assistant to those two techniques is this lesson's own framing, not a statement from MITRE.\n\n### What the analyst writes down\n\nFor every pivot, note the source record `Id`, the value you copied, the search you ran and the time range in UTC. A chain nobody can repeat is not evidence.",
        "codeExample": "# Claude Enterprise: one hour of file uploads and new chats (window polling)\ncurl --fail-with-body -sS -G \\\n  \"https://api.anthropic.com/v1/compliance/activities\" \\\n  -H \"x-api-key: $ANTHROPIC_COMPLIANCE_ACCESS_KEY\" \\\n  -H \"anthropic-version: 2026-10-21\" \\\n  --data-urlencode \"activity_types[]=claude_file_uploaded\" \\\n  --data-urlencode \"activity_types[]=claude_chat_created\" \\\n  --data-urlencode \"created_at.gte=2026-10-06T09:00:00Z\" \\\n  --data-urlencode \"created_at.lt=2026-10-06T10:00:00Z\" \\\n  --data-urlencode \"limit=5000\"\n\n# Pivot worksheet\n# user     -> actor.user_id      = user_01Rb6TcYn4JwQe8ZkLp2VxHs9D\n# session  -> claude_chat_id     = claude_chat_01Wm3ZpKc7RtNv5HxQy8BdLf2G\n# resource -> claude_file_id     = (from the claude_file_uploaded activity)\n# network  -> actor.ip_address   = 198.51.100.73  (search proxy and VPN logs)"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Worked case: who used the assistant with the Q4 price list?",
        "content": "### The first signal\n\nKettering Mills, a fictional manufacturer, runs Microsoft 365 Copilot for its office staff. On 8 October the procurement director writes to the SOC: a supplier seems to know the company's internal target prices. She asks one narrow question: who used the assistant with Supplier-Price-List-Q4.xlsx during the past week? This is a request to read a trail, not an accusation.\n\n### Step 1: start from the resource\n\nThe analyst has a file, not a person, so the search starts at the resource end of the chain. In Sentinel she expands `LLMEventData.AccessedResources` in `CopilotActivity` and filters on the file name for 1 to 8 October. Two accounts appear: m.adeyemi and p.lindqvist.\n\n### Step 2: read each record against the six questions\n\n| Question | m.adeyemi | p.lindqvist |\n|---|---|---|\n| When (UTC) | 6 October 08:41 | 7 October 13:05 |\n| From where | 198.51.100.73, region GB | 198.51.100.73, region GB |\n| In which app | Word | BizChat |\n| Which session | One `ThreadId`, three records | One `ThreadId`, one record |\n| What was touched | The price list, `Action` Read, `Status` success | The price list and two emails, all Read, success |\n| Label | `SensitivityLabelId` present | `SensitivityLabelId` present |\n\n198.51.100.73 is the office's outbound address, and both users also have IdP sign-ins from it on those days. The analyst does not rely on `ClientIP` alone: the sign-in log confirms it.\n\n### Step 3: follow the session\n\nShe searches each `ThreadId`. m.adeyemi's thread has three records in six minutes, all in Word with the supplier review document in `Contexts`. p.lindqvist's thread is a single BizChat prompt.\n\n### Step 4: leave the AI log\n\nUsing the `SiteUrl` from `AccessedResources`, she pulls SharePoint `FileAccessed` records for the same path. Nine people opened the file directly that week, without the assistant. The assistant was one of several routes to the file, and not the most used.\n\n### Step 5: state what the records cannot say\n\nThe audit records do not contain the prompts. She cannot say what either user asked, or whether target prices appeared in an answer. She checks retention: the events are days old, far inside 180 days, so nothing has aged out.\n\n### The decision\n\nShe reports to the director:\n\n- Two users had Copilot reference the file, on the dates and from the network shown.\n- Both are members of the procurement team that owns the file.\n- Nine other users opened the file directly.\n- No record shows the content leaving the organisation. These records could not show that.\n- Prompt and answer text exists in the users' mailboxes and can be requested through the compliance team if the director opens a formal case.\n\nShe classifies the SOC ticket as informational and attaches the record `Id` values and her queries.\n\n### Why this is a good outcome\n\nThe analyst answered the question that was asked, with the trail that exists, and marked the edge of the evidence. She did not turn two ordinary Copilot sessions into suspects. If the director opens a case, the next steps belong to the lessons on unauthorised data access and on leakage analysis.",
        "codeExample": "Case notes (all times UTC)\n\n2026-10-06 08:41:17  m.adeyemi   CopilotInteraction  AppHost=Word     Thread=19:Qm4t...@thread.v2  Read Supplier-Price-List-Q4.xlsx (label present)\n2026-10-06 08:44:02  m.adeyemi   CopilotInteraction  AppHost=Word     Thread=19:Qm4t...@thread.v2  no AccessedResources\n2026-10-06 08:47:30  m.adeyemi   CopilotInteraction  AppHost=Word     Thread=19:Qm4t...@thread.v2  Read Supplier-Price-List-Q4.xlsx\n2026-10-07 13:05:51  p.lindqvist CopilotInteraction  AppHost=BizChat  Thread=19:Zk9c...@thread.v2  Read Supplier-Price-List-Q4.xlsx + 2 emails\n\nSharePoint FileAccessed, same ObjectId, 1 to 8 October: 9 distinct users\nPrompt text: not in these records. Request through compliance if a case is opened.\nRetention check: events are under 180 days old.\nVerdict: informational. Record Ids and queries attached."
      }
